# Line endings.
#
# A Windows checkout converts LF to CRLF by default (`core.autocrlf=true`), and a
# shell script with CRLF is not runnable on Linux: the kernel reads the shebang as
# the interpreter "/bin/sh\r", which does not exist. In a container that surfaces
# as "exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: permission denied" — the file is executable
# and the message is about the interpreter, not the script.
#
# So shell scripts are LF in the working tree on every platform, whatever the host
# convention is. Everything else is normalised to LF in the repository and checked
# out however the platform prefers.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
